{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786457350,"nonce":"eYuATHcfk+a793mNOzXuPAPi5FI=","message":"**Closed: the premise of this issue was overtaken two hours after it was filed, and the issue was\nnever updated.** Split — see `2078a07` for the part that is still true.\n\n**Provenance.**\n\n| When | What |\n|---|---|\n| 2026-08-08 22:56 IST | this issue filed: \"No Slack credential has ever been used by this codebase\" |\n| 2026-08-09 00:54 IST | `a7cdec3` committed: \"fix: the Slack card defects found by running it for real\" — \"Every item here was observed in a live workspace, not inferred\" |\n\n`a7cdec3` is authored by the repo owner and predates the `tests/domain-coverage-and-harness` work\nby two days. It carries findings that oto's outbound validator cannot structurally produce: a\nduration reading \"under a second\" on a group that had been firing for 80s; \"Started\" losing 21\nminutes against upstream's `startsAt`; `DedupTTL` equalling `refire_grace` so an identical re-fire\nwas swallowed at ingest before re-fire logic could see it; and the colour bar rendering while the\nbuttons did not, \"confirmed by eye\". You do not find a 21-minute clock discrepancy by reading\n`validate.go`.\n\nSo a workspace *was* connected, roughly two hours after this issue said none ever had been. The\nsentence \"no workspace has ever been connected\" is false as of 2026-08-09 and this ticket has\nsimply been stale since.\n\n**Why this needs recording.** A later session read this open issue as current fact and concluded\nthat it and ADR 0020 Amendment 4 were in direct contradiction — that Amendment 4 described a live\nrun that never happened, and that nothing in the repo could decide which was lying. The repo could\ndecide it: the commit log does, on timestamps and on the content of the observations. An open\nticket is not evidence of present state, and a stale one nearly cost this project's own\ndocumentation its credibility.\n\n**What this issue got right, and what survives.** The reasoning was sound and mostly still holds —\nAmendment 4 exists *because* Block Kit fails in ways a local validator cannot see, which is exactly\nwhat this issue predicted. But the live run settled one question only: what a `thread_broadcast`\ndoes with attachments, colour and buttons. The threading model, `chat.update` in place (ADR 0008's\ncentral claim), a mention that actually notifies, the resolved and snoozed variants, four-client\nparity, and captures landing in the repo are all still unobserved. Those move to `2078a07`, which\nstays open and stays blocked on a human with a workspace.","files":null}]}